  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Consider $P(x)=ax^4+bx^3+cx^2+dx+e$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that if $P(x)$ is an even function, then $b=d=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(-x)=P(x)$ gives $-b=b$ and $-d=d$, so $b=d=0$.",
          "worked": "For an even function, $P(-x)=P(x)$ for every $x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-x)&=ax^4-bx^3+cx^2-dx+e\\\\ax^4-bx^3+cx^2-dx+e&=ax^4+bx^3+cx^2+dx+e\\end{align*}$$\nComparing coefficients of $x^3$ and $x$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}-b&=b\\\\b&=0\\\\-d&=d\\\\d&=0\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Using your result from part a, explain why for an even polynomial, if $x=\\alpha$ is a root, then $x=-\\alpha$ is also a root.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Only even powers remain, so $P(-\\alpha)=P(\\alpha)=0$.",
          "worked": "From part a, $P(x)=ax^4+cx^2+e$. Since $\\alpha$ is a root, $P(\\alpha)=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-\\alpha)&=a(-\\alpha)^4+c(-\\alpha)^2+e\\\\&=a\\alpha^4+c\\alpha^2+e\\\\&=P(\\alpha)\\\\&=0\\end{align*}$$\nHence $-\\alpha$ is also a root.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
    ]
  },
